{"requirements":[{"id":"ssh-ca-host-certificate","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1 and configure the SSH host to present a certificate signed by it.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ed25519 CA and host certificate were created with node1 and its IP as principals. sshd was configured with the certificate, reloaded successfully, and directly verified after reboot as active, valid, and using that HostCertificate.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c31d9721abea4307","cmd-70f233585628492f","cmd-deccf68fccc04788"]},{"id":"ca-trusted-client-connection","requirement":"Allow a client that trusts the CA to connect without pinning node1's individual host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The test client used an @cert-authority-only known_hosts file, disabled global host entries, required the certificate host-key algorithm, matched the presented certificate to the CA, and completed authenticated SSH connections before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-db46e9eb622744db","cmd-d0d3750bb6e549a9","cmd-ef3d23616a3c431a","cmd-409cf4f3d832449e","cmd-deccf68fccc04788"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"Every state-changing command had a clear role in implementation, safe validation, final evidence collection, or the required restart protocol; no causally unnecessary troubleshooting mutation was identified. Hygiene is reduced by retained validation residue: cmd-db46e9eb622744db created a test client keypair, CA trust file, and an additional authorized_keys entry with no captured cleanup, while cmd-deccf68fccc04788 left post-restart debug/output files in /tmp, confirmed by the final temporary-files snapshot. These were bounded but should have been removed after verification. No failed services or package damage were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-db46e9eb622744db","cmd-deccf68fccc04788","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: healthy pre-restart service and CA-trusted SSH, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and recovered certificate-based SSH afterward.","limitations":["The client verification was performed from node1 back to its own network address rather than from a separate client node, although it still directly exercised strict CA-based SSH host verification.","The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /etc/ssh or the user's home directory, so final persistence there relies on the explicit post-restart checks and the absence of any captured cleanup of test-client artifacts."]}
